  <text key="guide.mode">Guided input</text>
  <text key="guide.startTitle">Bring me to the light</text>
  <text key="guide.startBody">No panic. We build the calls step by step. But you definitely need a towel.</text>
  <text key="guide.dicomTitle">Find DicomServices</text>
  <text key="guide.dicomBody">Where does DicomServices live? A clean FQDN, and we are in business.</text>
  <text key="guide.viewerTitle">Sort the Viewer address</text>
  <text key="guide.viewerBody">Same FQDN? Lovely. If not, the Viewer gets its own address here.</text>
  <text key="guide.viewerFqdnTitle">Enter the DU Viewer</text>
  <text key="guide.viewerFqdnBody">If the Viewer has a different name than DicomServices, put its FQDN here.</text>
  <text key="guide.idpTitle">IDP, if one joins in</text>
  <text key="guide.idpBody">If an identity provider is involved, enter its name here. Otherwise this field may stay empty.</text>
  <text key="guide.sharedTitle">Shared user: yes or no?</text>
  <text key="guide.sharedBody">Using a URL shared user? Then this is where it gets its moment.</text>
  <text key="guide.sharedUserTitle">The shared user itself</text>
  <text key="guide.sharedUserBody">If the field is empty, we set du.webviewer.rl. Handy, but use it deliberately.</text>
  <text key="guide.sharedIdpTitle">Is the shared user in the IDP?</text>
  <text key="guide.sharedIdpBody">Only enable this when the shared user really lives in the IDP. Otherwise keep the door closed.</text>
  <text key="guide.sharedPasswordTitle">Secret for the shared user</text>
  <text key="guide.sharedPasswordBody">The shared password goes here. Not poetic, but important.</text>
  <text key="guide.browserTitle">Choose browser</text>
  <text key="guide.browserBody">System default is relaxed. Pick Chrome or Edge only when the call should deliberately go there.</text>
  <text key="guide.companionTitle">Companion App ready?</text>
  <text key="guide.companionBody">If Companion App calls are needed, it needs its folder path to happiness here.</text>
  <text key="guide.kisTitle">Which system is calling?</text>
  <text key="guide.kisBody">ORBIS or external HIS? This decides which special fields appear later.</text>
  <text key="guide.terminalTitle">Terminal HIS?</text>
  <text key="guide.terminalBody">If ORBIS runs in terminal, Citrix or RDP, we set the remote note and matching parameter.</text>
  <text key="guide.foreignTitle">External RIS variables</text>
  <text key="guide.foreignBody">For an external HIS, variables need clear names. Otherwise the call does not know what to replace.</text>
  <text key="guide.userTitle">Test user</text>
  <text key="guide.userBody">To test calls directly, the test calls need a user.</text>
  <text key="guide.passwordTitle">Test password</text>
  <text key="guide.passwordBody">And here is the password for the test user. Visible, so you can check it.</text>
  <text key="guide.patientTitle">Patient ID</text>
  <text key="guide.patientBody">The patient ID is the first signpost to the right studies.</text>
  <text key="guide.orderTitle">Order number</text>
  <text key="guide.orderBody">Put the order number here. Multiple values work by comma, semicolon or one per line.</text>
  <text key="guide.studyTitle">SUID</text>
  <text key="guide.studyBody">Finally the StudyUID, so the study call lands exactly where it should.</text>
  <text key="guide.doneTitle">Light reached</text>
  <text key="guide.doneBody">All set. I will show you the finished calls now.</text>
</translations>
